1
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:23,408
Special Agent Owen Carrick was, at forty-two, exactly
the kind of man the Federal Bureau

2
00:00:23,428 --> 00:00:30,112
of Investigation was designed to produce: competent,
careful, and one promotion away from

3
00:00:30,132 --> 00:00:30,941
Washington.

4
00:00:31,510 --> 00:00:37,593
He had joined the Bureau at twenty-nine, after four
years as a prosecutor in Harrisburg.

5
00:00:37,613 --> 00:00:43,765
He had worked public corruption, and then
counter-terrorism, and then — because it looked

6
00:00:43,785 --> 00:00:49,659
good on a file, and because nobody else in the
field office wanted it — he had become

7
00:00:49,679 --> 00:00:55,553
the Weapons of Mass Destruction coordinator for
eastern Pennsylvania and Delaware. It

8
00:00:55,573 --> 00:01:02,141
was a job that mostly involved hazmat drills, and
lectures to university chemistry departments,

9
00:01:02,161 --> 00:01:08,313
and anthrax hoaxes, and one man in Scranton who kept
trying to buy ricin on the internet.

10
00:01:08,882 --> 00:01:14,462
He lived in Media, Pennsylvania, with his wife
Laura, who taught eighth-grade science,

11
00:01:14,482 --> 00:01:20,451
and two daughters, seventeen and eight. He had applied
that summer for a unit chief position

12
00:01:20,471 --> 00:01:26,116
at the Weapons of Mass Destruction Directorate at
headquarters, and the interview board

13
00:01:26,136 --> 00:01:32,040
was in November, and he thought he had a good chance,
and he had not told anyone but Laura.

14
00:01:32,611 --> 00:01:39,336
He had not asked for the Umbrella case. It had come
to him the way cases come to men in

15
00:01:39,356 --> 00:01:46,391
his position — as a referral from another agency,
forwarded by a supervisor with a one-line

16
00:01:46,411 --> 00:01:52,517
note. CDC plus FDA. Possible select agent issue.
Probably nothing. Take a look.

17
00:01:54,087 --> 00:02:00,266
Probably nothing. That was what Owen Carrick believed,
on the fourteenth of August, twenty

18
00:02:00,286 --> 00:02:06,603
twenty-nine, when he drove to Wilmington to serve a
grand jury subpoena on a biotech company

19
00:02:06,623 --> 00:02:10,597
whose chief executive had been on the
cover of a magazine.

20
00:02:11,168 --> 00:02:17,168
It was the last time in his life he would believe
anything about Albert Wesker so comfortably.

21
00:02:19,237 --> 00:02:24,977
Doctor Samira Haddad flew up from Atlanta in
September for the joint site visit.

22
00:02:25,548 --> 00:02:30,735
They met in the arrivals hall, and disliked each
other on sight, in the way that people

23
00:02:30,755 --> 00:02:36,003
from different professions often do. She thought he
looked like a man who measured cases

24
00:02:36,023 --> 00:02:41,390
by what they would do for his career. He thought she
looked like a scientist who had never

25
00:02:41,410 --> 00:02:43,724
had to put anything in front of a jury.

26
00:02:44,294 --> 00:02:49,594
They were, it would turn out, both right. And they
would, over the next seventeen years,

27
00:02:49,614 --> 00:02:52,978
become the closest thing either of
them had to a friend.

28
00:02:54,468 --> 00:02:55,789
You've read the complaint?

29
00:02:56,310 --> 00:03:01,663
I've read it. I've read the photographs. I've read
a table of monkeys and a note in a

30
00:03:01,683 --> 00:03:07,415
margin. I've also read that this company's biggest
product is a drug that has three hundred

31
00:03:07,435 --> 00:03:12,788
senior citizens riding motorcycles, and that its
CEO is personally friends with — I'm

32
00:03:12,808 --> 00:03:18,034
sorry, is personally photographed with — half the
senators on the Health Committee.

33
00:03:18,555 --> 00:03:19,742
That's not an answer.

34
00:03:20,263 --> 00:03:23,729
It's the context. I have to know
what I'm walking into.

35
00:03:24,250 --> 00:03:29,476
You're walking into a building where somebody wrote
Select accordingly next to nineteen

36
00:03:29,496 --> 00:03:30,320
dead primates.

37
00:03:31,840 --> 00:03:37,386
Carrick did not answer. He looked at the road. And
somewhere at the back of his mind —

38
00:03:37,406 --> 00:03:43,211
the part that had once been a prosecutor, before it
had become a careerist — a small light

39
00:03:43,231 --> 00:03:43,729
came on.

40
00:03:45,799 --> 00:03:51,697
The records had arrived two weeks before. Forty-one
thousand pages, in forty-three boxes,

41
00:03:51,717 --> 00:03:57,415
delivered to the field office by a courier van on
the exact day the subpoena required,

42
00:03:57,435 --> 00:04:03,466
indexed, cross-referenced, and bound. Shipping logs.
Animal care records. Facility permits.

43
00:04:03,486 --> 00:04:09,185
Power bills. Catherine Adeyemi's team had produced
everything that had been asked for,

44
00:04:09,205 --> 00:04:13,574
and a great deal that had not, and they
had produced it perfectly.

45
00:04:14,144 --> 00:04:20,005
It was, Carrick's supervisor said, the most
cooperative subpoena response he had seen

46
00:04:20,025 --> 00:04:21,113
in thirty years.

47
00:04:21,682 --> 00:04:27,684
It was also, Carrick thought — looking at the
forty-three boxes stacked against his cubicle

48
00:04:27,704 --> 00:04:33,375
wall — exactly what a company would do if it wanted
to bury an investigation in paper.

49
00:04:35,444 --> 00:04:38,016
The Director met them at the door himself.

50
00:04:38,586 --> 00:04:43,967
He had not been asked to. The visit had been
arranged through lawyers, as such visits

51
00:04:43,987 --> 00:04:49,369
always are. A corporate counsel would escort them,
and a compliance officer, and they

52
00:04:49,389 --> 00:04:54,834
would be shown the relevant facilities, and they
would be given coffee in a conference

53
00:04:54,854 --> 00:04:56,613
room. That was the protocol.

54
00:04:57,184 --> 00:05:03,373
Instead, at nine o'clock exactly, as the government
sedan pulled into the visitors' bay,

55
00:05:03,393 --> 00:05:09,512
Albert Wesker walked out through the glass doors of
the tower, alone, and opened Samira

56
00:05:09,532 --> 00:05:11,347
Haddad's car door for her.

57
00:05:12,036 --> 00:05:17,803
Doctor Haddad. Welcome to Delaware. I read your
paper on the Nipah cluster in Kerala.

58
00:05:17,823 --> 00:05:23,997
The contact-tracing model in section four was elegant.
Agent Carrick — thank you for coming

59
00:05:24,017 --> 00:05:27,945
yourself. I know how many hazmat
drills you had to cancel.

60
00:05:29,266 --> 00:05:34,977
Carrick shook the hand he was offered. It was cool
and dry and very strong, and it held

61
00:05:34,997 --> 00:05:38,931
his a fraction of a second longer than
a handshake needs to.

62
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:45,605
He had been ready for lawyers. He had been ready for
obstruction, for delay, for a conference

63
00:05:45,625 --> 00:05:51,203
room with bad coffee. He was not ready for this —
the chief executive himself, at the

64
00:05:51,223 --> 00:05:55,418
kerb, charming, unhurried, and apparently
delighted to see them.

65
00:05:55,987 --> 00:06:01,873
That was the point. Hospitality is a weapon. It is
the only weapon that the person you

66
00:06:01,893 --> 00:06:03,865
use it on will thank you for.

67
00:06:05,935 --> 00:06:07,506
He gave them everything.

68
00:06:08,076 --> 00:06:13,547
He gave them six hours, and every floor of the tower,
and every door they asked to open,

69
00:06:13,567 --> 00:06:19,039
and several they had not thought to ask about. He
took them through the assay programme,

70
00:06:19,059 --> 00:06:24,656
and the Aeterna trial ward, where Arthur Kowalczyk —
who happened to be in for his monthly

71
00:06:24,676 --> 00:06:30,085
check-up that morning — told Samira Haddad at some
length about his motorcycle. He took

72
00:06:30,105 --> 00:06:35,639
them to the loading dock, and introduced them to
Deshawn. He showed them the power bills.

73
00:06:36,209 --> 00:06:42,040
And then, when Haddad raised the photograph — the
grey figure in the capsule — he took

74
00:06:42,060 --> 00:06:47,958
them to the lobby of the Singapore annex on the
tower's ground floor, where, in a glass

75
00:06:47,978 --> 00:06:53,808
case, beneath a spotlight, stood a nine-foot
sculpture of a grey, hairless figure in a

76
00:06:53,828 --> 00:06:57,481
frosted cylinder, with a small brass
plaque beside it.

77
00:06:58,052 --> 00:07:04,677
The Sentinel. A commission by a young sculptor from
Rhode Island, for Umbrella's campaign

78
00:07:04,697 --> 00:07:09,829
on biosecurity. Resin, steel, and acrylic.
Two thousand twenty-eight.

79
00:07:11,519 --> 00:07:13,846
You're telling me that's what's in
the photograph.

80
00:07:14,365 --> 00:07:20,501
I'm telling you that's what Doctor Okafor
photographed. Doctor Marsh's team used a picture

81
00:07:20,521 --> 00:07:27,066
of it on a slide about public perception. The label on
the page is a working title. T-zero-zero.

82
00:07:27,086 --> 00:07:31,101
The zero-zero is because the sculptor
hadn't signed it yet.

83
00:07:32,221 --> 00:07:38,254
Pell had commissioned the sculpture in April, from a
real artist, who had been paid real

84
00:07:38,274 --> 00:07:44,306
money and would go on to have a real career. It had
taken her ten weeks. The invoice was

85
00:07:44,326 --> 00:07:45,681
in box twenty-seven.

86
00:07:47,752 --> 00:07:49,506
The macaques were Evelyn's.

87
00:07:50,076 --> 00:07:56,254
She met them in a conference room on the ninth floor,
in a crisp white coat, with a folder,

88
00:07:56,274 --> 00:08:02,179
and she explained — clearly, patiently, in the tone
of a scientist explaining something

89
00:08:02,199 --> 00:08:08,309
slightly embarrassing to a colleague — that the
twenty-four rhesus macaques from Singapore

90
00:08:08,329 --> 00:08:14,099
had been part of a standard toxicology study for
Aeterna, conducted under contract by

91
00:08:14,119 --> 00:08:20,297
a licensed research organisation in Maryland. The
contract was in box fifteen. The Maryland

92
00:08:20,317 --> 00:08:23,635
facility's inspection reports
were in box twelve.

93
00:08:24,324 --> 00:08:29,936
In a toxicology study the animals are humanely
euthanised at the end of the dosing period

94
00:08:29,956 --> 00:08:35,504
so that their tissues can be examined. That's what
deceased means in the table. Nineteen

95
00:08:35,524 --> 00:08:41,009
at the scheduled end-point. The five marked enhanced
were an enhanced-monitoring cohort

96
00:08:41,029 --> 00:08:46,450
— animals kept for a longer observation window.
They're still alive. You're welcome to

97
00:08:46,470 --> 00:08:47,147
visit them.

98
00:08:47,666 --> 00:08:50,490
And the note in the margin?
Select accordingly?

99
00:08:51,009 --> 00:08:56,562
Group housing. Macaques are social, and it's better
for their welfare if we house them

100
00:08:56,582 --> 00:09:02,134
together, but animals with high baseline aggression
don't tolerate co-housing well. So

101
00:09:02,154 --> 00:09:08,095
we select the calmer ones for the group enclosures.
It's in our animal welfare plan. Section

102
00:09:08,115 --> 00:09:08,355
six.

103
00:09:10,075 --> 00:09:16,570
It was a good answer. It was, in fact, word for
word, a real section of a real animal

104
00:09:16,590 --> 00:09:23,316
welfare plan, which Pell had written and backdated in
March, the week after Nadia Okafor

105
00:09:23,336 --> 00:09:24,005
resigned.

106
00:09:24,576 --> 00:09:30,536
Samira Haddad wrote it down. She had been an epidemic
intelligence officer for four years,

107
00:09:30,556 --> 00:09:36,251
and she knew what she was looking at: an answer to
every question, before the question

108
00:09:36,271 --> 00:09:42,099
had been asked. It did not mean they were lying. It
meant they had known she was coming.

109
00:09:42,669 --> 00:09:44,278
Which, of course, they had.

110
00:09:46,346 --> 00:09:52,268
The last stop was the old building. Haddad had
insisted on it. The complaint had mentioned

111
00:09:52,288 --> 00:09:58,275
a sublevel, and the power bills had been
extraordinary, and she wanted to see the basement.

112
00:09:58,846 --> 00:10:04,367
So they went down in the freight elevator — the
Director, the two investigators, and a

113
00:10:04,387 --> 00:10:09,909
tall man in a black suit who had joined them without
introduction in the lobby and who

114
00:10:09,929 --> 00:10:14,678
stood in the corner of the elevator with his
hands folded in front of him.

115
00:10:15,248 --> 00:10:21,003
The elevator panel had two buttons. Loading dock.
Basement. Carrick looked at it for a

116
00:10:21,023 --> 00:10:26,845
long moment. There was no third button. There was a
smooth, blank steel plate below the

117
00:10:26,865 --> 00:10:32,755
basement button, with four screws in it, and a very
faint rectangular mark in the paint,

118
00:10:32,775 --> 00:10:35,240
as though something had been removed.

119
00:10:35,809 --> 00:10:41,767
The basement had the twenty old freezers, and six
new ones, and the vault that Pell had

120
00:10:41,787 --> 00:10:47,951
bought for eighteen hundred Points to show Catherine
eighteen months before. It was clean,

121
00:10:47,971 --> 00:10:54,272
and cold, and it used, the Director explained, a great
deal of power, because it was holding

122
00:10:54,292 --> 00:11:00,181
thirty years of biological samples at minus one
hundred and fifty degrees, and because

123
00:11:00,201 --> 00:11:04,440
the old building's electrical system
was, frankly, a disgrace.

124
00:11:05,009 --> 00:11:09,854
The corner where the grey machine had stood was
bare concrete, freshly painted.

125
00:11:11,744 --> 00:11:12,897
What was here before?

126
00:11:13,417 --> 00:11:19,581
An air handling unit. Arcturus. We replaced it last
year. The state inspected it in twenty

127
00:11:19,601 --> 00:11:24,461
twenty-six, if you'd like the report. Mister
Delgado was very thorough.

128
00:11:25,980 --> 00:11:31,782
He had said the name of the inspector. Carrick
wrote it down. He did not know why. He

129
00:11:31,802 --> 00:11:37,603
would find the report, four months later, in a
state database, and read it, and begin

130
00:11:37,623 --> 00:11:43,493
to wonder why a small research lab in twenty
twenty-six had gone so far out of its way

131
00:11:43,513 --> 00:11:44,588
to be inspected.

132
00:11:46,658 --> 00:11:51,725
It was at the very end of the day, in the lobby,
that Owen Carrick noticed the thing he

133
00:11:51,745 --> 00:11:54,474
would think about for the next
seventeen years.

134
00:11:55,043 --> 00:12:00,790
He was waiting for Haddad, who had gone to the
restroom. The Director had excused himself

135
00:12:00,810 --> 00:12:06,427
to take a call. And the tall man in the black suit —
the one who had ridden down in the

136
00:12:06,447 --> 00:12:12,258
elevator with them without being introduced — was
standing by the lifts, twenty feet away,

137
00:12:12,278 --> 00:12:16,015
with his hands folded in front of him,
looking at nothing.

138
00:12:16,586 --> 00:12:22,113
Carrick watched him. He did not know why. It was a
cop's habit, from long before he had

139
00:12:22,133 --> 00:12:27,150
become a careerist — you watch the person in the
room who isn't doing anything.

140
00:12:27,719 --> 00:12:30,963
He watched him for a minute. Then
two. Then three.

141
00:12:33,033 --> 00:12:36,638
In three minutes, the man in the black
suit did not blink.

142
00:12:37,208 --> 00:12:43,035
Carrick knew it was not possible. A human being
blinks between fifteen and twenty times

143
00:12:43,055 --> 00:12:48,748
a minute. You can stop yourself for a while —
thirty seconds, perhaps a minute if you

144
00:12:48,768 --> 00:12:54,594
concentrate — but your eyes begin to water and burn.
This man's eyes were not watering.

145
00:12:54,614 --> 00:13:00,441
They were perfectly clear and perfectly still, and
he was not concentrating on anything

146
00:13:00,461 --> 00:13:00,911
at all.

147
00:13:01,480 --> 00:13:07,106
At three minutes and forty seconds, as if he had felt
himself being counted, the man turned

148
00:13:07,126 --> 00:13:10,643
his head and looked directly at
Carrick across the lobby.

149
00:13:11,212 --> 00:13:14,146
Then he blinked. Once. Slowly.
Like a courtesy.

150
00:13:16,216 --> 00:13:21,728
The Director walked them to their car. He opened the
door for Haddad again. He thanked

151
00:13:21,748 --> 00:13:27,260
them both, sincerely, for their time and their
diligence, and said that Umbrella would

152
00:13:27,280 --> 00:13:32,728
cooperate with anything else the government needed,
and that he hoped they would come

153
00:13:32,748 --> 00:13:33,049
back.

154
00:13:33,620 --> 00:13:39,393
Then, as Carrick walked around to the driver's side,
the Director said, quite pleasantly,

155
00:13:39,413 --> 00:13:41,671
in a voice pitched for no one else:

156
00:13:42,361 --> 00:13:48,269
Good luck with the unit chief board in November, Agent
Carrick. I hear it's a strong field.

157
00:13:50,288 --> 00:13:52,899
Carrick stopped with his hand on
the car door.

158
00:13:53,467 --> 00:13:59,441
He had told no one. Not his supervisor. Not his
partner. Not his friends. He had told

159
00:13:59,461 --> 00:14:04,658
his wife, at their kitchen table, one night in
June, with the window open.

160
00:14:05,229 --> 00:14:11,036
He looked up. The Director was already walking back
toward the glass doors, unhurried,

161
00:14:11,056 --> 00:14:14,763
hands in the pockets of his coat. He
did not look back.

162
00:14:17,582 --> 00:14:21,573
Assessment. Federal inspection
neutralised.

163
00:14:24,913 --> 00:14:28,802
They drove north on the interstate in
silence for twenty minutes.

164
00:14:29,491 --> 00:14:32,766
Well. That was the cleanest facility I
have ever inspected.

165
00:14:33,287 --> 00:14:33,950
Yeah.

166
00:14:34,470 --> 00:14:40,066
Every answer ready. Every document in a numbered
box. A sculpture, for God's sake. Owen

167
00:14:40,086 --> 00:14:42,519
— they knew we were coming for months.

168
00:14:43,039 --> 00:14:48,185
They knew we were coming for months because we sent
them a subpoena, Samira. That's not

169
00:14:48,205 --> 00:14:48,660
a crime.

170
00:14:50,181 --> 00:14:51,307
So you're closing it.

171
00:14:53,326 --> 00:14:58,970
He should have. Every instinct that had got him to
where he was — every instinct that

172
00:14:58,990 --> 00:15:05,300
was about to get him to Washington — told him to close
it. A cooperative company. A disgruntled

173
00:15:05,320 --> 00:15:11,231
former employee. A clean inspection. A sculpture in a
glass case. A file that would look,

174
00:15:11,251 --> 00:15:16,961
to an interview board in November, like a careful
agent doing careful work and knowing

175
00:15:16,981 --> 00:15:17,828
when to stop.

176
00:15:18,519 --> 00:15:19,724
He didn't blink.

177
00:15:20,244 --> 00:15:20,798
What?

178
00:15:21,317 --> 00:15:26,782
The guard. The big one, by the lifts. I watched him
for almost four minutes. He didn't

179
00:15:26,802 --> 00:15:27,484
blink once.

180
00:15:29,004 --> 00:15:30,519
That's — people can do that.

181
00:15:31,039 --> 00:15:32,225
No. They can't.

182
00:15:34,245 --> 00:15:39,992
He drove on in silence. The rain began, and he
turned on the wipers, and watched them

183
00:15:40,012 --> 00:15:40,807
for a while.

184
00:15:41,376 --> 00:15:47,530
He did not close the file. He did not decide not to
close it, either. He simply did not

185
00:15:47,550 --> 00:15:53,846
close it that night, or the next, and the file sat in
the tray on his desk, under a stack

186
00:15:53,866 --> 00:15:56,684
of hazmat drill reports, for eight days.

187
00:15:57,254 --> 00:16:03,436
On the twelfth day, a man called the Philadelphia field
office from a pay phone at a highway

188
00:16:03,456 --> 00:16:09,032
rest stop, and asked for Special Agent Carrick by
name, and would not give his own.
